3 Uninfluenceable and unriggable reward-function learning processes
--------------------------------------------------------------------



What would constitute a ‘good’ reward-function learning process?
In [Figure 2](#S2.F2 "Figure 2 ‣ 2.1 Reward functions and learning processes ‣ 2 Notation and formalism ‣ Pitfalls of learning a reward function online1footnote 11footnote 1Copyright International Joint Conferences on Artificial Intelligence (IJCAI). All rights reserved."), the reward function R𝑅Ritalic\_R is a causal descendant of hnsubscriptℎ𝑛h\_{n}italic\_h start\_POSTSUBSCRIPT italic\_n end\_POSTSUBSCRIPT, which itself is a causal descendant of the agent’s policy π𝜋\piitalic\_π.




Ideally, we do not want the reward function to be a causal descendant of the policy.
Instead, we want it to be specified by the environment, as shown in the causal graph of [Figure 3](#S3.F3 "Figure 3 ‣ 3 Uninfluenceable and unriggable reward-function learning processes ‣ Pitfalls of learning a reward function online1footnote 11footnote 1Copyright International Joint Conferences on Artificial Intelligence (IJCAI). All rights reserved.") (similar to the graphs in Everitt and Hutter ([2019](#bib.bib11))).
There, the reward function R𝑅Ritalic\_R is no longer a causal descendant of hnsubscriptℎ𝑛h\_{n}italic\_h start\_POSTSUBSCRIPT italic\_n end\_POSTSUBSCRIPT, and thus not of π𝜋\piitalic\_π.




Instead, it is a function of μ𝜇\muitalic\_μ, and of the node η𝜂\etaitalic\_η, which gives a probability distribution over reward functions, conditional on environments.
This is written as P(R∣η,μ)𝑃conditional𝑅𝜂𝜇P(R\mid\eta,\mu)italic\_P ( italic\_R ∣ italic\_η , italic\_μ ).



