Theorem 0 (No sacrifice ↭↭\leftrightsquigarrow↭ unriggable).



The reward-function learning process ρ𝜌{\rho}italic\_ρ is riggable if and only if there exists
an affine relabeling σ:ℛ→ℛnormal-:𝜎normal-→ℛℛ\sigma:{\mathcal{R}}\to{\mathcal{R}}italic\_σ : caligraphic\_R → caligraphic\_R such that
πσ∘ρsuperscript𝜋𝜎𝜌\pi^{\sigma\circ{\rho}}italic\_π start\_POSTSUPERSCRIPT italic\_σ ∘ italic\_ρ end\_POSTSUPERSCRIPT (the policy that optimises the value of σ∘ρ𝜎𝜌\sigma\circ{\rho}italic\_σ ∘ italic\_ρ) sacrifices reward with certainty (is strictly inferior to another policy on a given history, for all possible reward functions in the image of σ∘ρ𝜎𝜌\sigma\circ{\rho}italic\_σ ∘ italic\_ρ).





So any riggable ρ𝜌{\rho}italic\_ρ is just one relabelling of its rewards away from sacrificing reward with certainty.
It can also exhibit other pathologies, like refusing to learn; see [Section 6](#S6 "6 Experiments ‣ Pitfalls of learning a reward function online1footnote 11footnote 1Copyright International Joint Conferences on Artificial Intelligence (IJCAI). All rights reserved.").





#### 
4.3.4 Example of reward sacrifice



For an easy example of this behaviour, consider the learning process of [Section 3.5](#S3.SS5 "3.5 Riggable example ‣ 3 Uninfluenceable and unriggable reward-function learning processes ‣ Pitfalls of learning a reward function online1footnote 11footnote 1Copyright International Joint Conferences on Artificial Intelligence (IJCAI). All rights reserved."), with one modification: asking the mother (a1=Msubscript𝑎1𝑀a\_{1}=Mitalic\_a start\_POSTSUBSCRIPT 1 end\_POSTSUBSCRIPT = italic\_M) gets an extra penalty of 1111 for both reward functions, RBsubscript𝑅𝐵R\_{B}italic\_R start\_POSTSUBSCRIPT italic\_B end\_POSTSUBSCRIPT and RDsubscript𝑅𝐷R\_{D}italic\_R start\_POSTSUBSCRIPT italic\_D end\_POSTSUBSCRIPT.




