We train our agents’ policies, and the value of these policies, via Q-learning (Sutton and
Barto, [1998](#bib.bib25)).
The exploration rate is ϵ=0.1italic-ϵ0.1\epsilon=0.1italic\_ϵ = 0.1, the agent is run for at most ten steps each episode. We use a learning rate of 1/n1𝑛1/n1 / italic\_n. Here n𝑛nitalic\_n is not the number of episodes the agent has taken, but the number of times the agent has been in state s𝑠sitalic\_s and taken action a𝑎aitalic\_a so far—hence the number of times it has updated Q(s,a)𝑄𝑠𝑎Q(s,a)italic\_Q ( italic\_s , italic\_a ). So each Q-value has a different n𝑛nitalic\_n. For each setup, we run Q-learning 1000100010001000 times. We graph the average Q-values for the resulting polices as well as one standard deviation around them.




